<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>

<!--
	Who may see and use a stored agent (tl.ai.core:Agent) or skill (tl.ai.core:Skill), in addition
	to <agent-access> (Ticket #30493). Both must allow.

	Every run and every list asks the model rights of the account: a stored agent or skill that the
	account may not read is unknown to it (AgentDefinitionQuery with a reader). This file gives the
	two base types their access definition:

	  1. The two base types inherit the roles held on the security root. So a role on the root keeps
	     its rights on each stored agent and skill, for example a role of the application that is
	     granted Write here.
	  2. The group "user" (role SecurityStructure.user on the root) gets Read. Without rules of the
	     application, stored agents and skills stay visible for every member of that group, as
	     before. This also lets the members read the instructions (systemPrompt) of an agent and the
	     text (content) of a skill, also with TL-Script or a data tool of an agent. An application
	     that hides these texts adds a <part> grant on the two attributes.
	  3. Nobody but an administrator changes the MCP servers and the infrastructure mark of a stored
	     agent: every run of the agent reaches the tools of its MCP servers, and the engine runs an
	     infrastructure agent in its own code. An application gives Write on the two attributes to
	     other roles with a <grant> of its own on the same <part>.

	Nothing is inherited. A specialization of the base types starts closed: without rules of the
	application, nobody but an administrator reads it. A specialization in a composition follows its
	container (the default access parent of a composition part), so the agents of a project are read
	and changed by whoever reads and changes the project.

	Create, Write and Delete of the base types are granted to nobody. Who may create an agent where
	is a model right that each application writes into its own security configuration.

	Administrators and code in a system context bypass the check (SecurityConfigurationService
	.isAllowedBypass); nothing here grants them anything.

	See the developer articles ai/security/run-access and ai/security/administration (the shipped
	access definition for stored agents and skills).
-->
<application xmlns:config="http://www.top-logic.com/ns/config/6.0">
	<services>
		<!--
			Contributed additively to whatever access manager the application runs; no "class" is
			named, for the reason tl-ai-core.binarySecurity.config.xml gives.

			Not inherited: an inherited role source would switch off the default access parent of a
			specialization held in a composition.
		-->
		<config service-class="com.top_logic.tool.boundsec.manager.AccessManager">
			<instance>
				<security-parents>
					<rule id="tl.ai.core.agent.root"
						inherit="false"
						meta-element="tl.ai.core:Agent"
					>
						<singleton module="SecurityStructure"/>
					</rule>
					<rule id="tl.ai.core.skill.root"
						inherit="false"
						meta-element="tl.ai.core:Skill"
					>
						<singleton module="SecurityStructure"/>
					</rule>
				</security-parents>
			</instance>
		</config>

		<config service-class="com.top_logic.model.security.SecurityConfigurationService">
			<instance>
				<security-config>
					<class name="tl.ai.core:Agent">
						<grant
							inherit="false"
							operation="Read"
							roles="SecurityStructure.user"
						/>
					</class>
					<class name="tl.ai.core:Skill">
						<grant
							inherit="false"
							operation="Read"
							roles="SecurityStructure.user"
						/>
					</class>
					<!-- A Write grant naming no role: nobody but an administrator writes it. -->
					<part name="tl.ai.core:Agent#mcpServers">
						<grant operation="Write"/>
					</part>
					<part name="tl.ai.core:Agent#infrastructure">
						<grant operation="Write"/>
					</part>
				</security-config>
			</instance>
		</config>
	</services>
</application>
